WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.028
Private RAG systems need observability because impressive demos can still return incomplete, stale, or misleading answers.

2
00:00:08.028 --> 00:00:18.982
Teams need to know whether retrieval finds the right sources, whether answers are grounded, whether citations are useful, and whether sensitive data is handled correctly.

3
00:00:18.982 --> 00:00:22.584
Evaluation should cover both retrieval and generation.

4
00:00:22.584 --> 00:00:31.962
Retrieval metrics may track relevant source coverage, filter behavior, ranking quality, duplicate results, no-answer outcomes, and freshness.

5
00:00:31.962 --> 00:00:42.541
Answer review may look at groundedness, citation coverage, completeness, tone, uncertainty handling, and whether the response stays inside the approved workflow.

6
00:00:42.541 --> 00:00:45.542
Evaluation sets make quality more repeatable.

7
00:00:45.542 --> 00:00:54.171
Teams can collect representative questions, expected source references, known edge cases, and workflow-specific success criteria.

8
00:00:54.171 --> 00:01:02.949
Running those checks before and after prompt changes, model changes, source changes, or index rebuilds helps prevent quiet regressions.

9
00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:15.779
Logging needs discipline. Prompt and response logs can help debug quality, but they may contain confidential source material, personal data, customer information, or sensitive operational details.

10
00:01:15.779 --> 00:01:25.383
Observability should include redaction, sampling, retention limits, access control, and privacy review rather than storing everything by default.

11
00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:30.560
User feedback and expert review are valuable when they are connected to a process.

12
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.187
Feedback should help improve sources, chunking, ranking, prompts, and documentation.

13
00:01:36.187 --> 00:01:44.515
Quality control is continuous because private RAG changes whenever the sources, index, model, permissions, or workflow changes.

14
00:01:44.515 --> 00:01:49.017
Useful dashboards should separate system health from answer quality.

15
00:01:49.017 --> 00:02:01.472
Latency, errors, and job status show whether the pipeline is running, while groundedness, citation usefulness, no-answer rates, and reviewer findings show whether the workflow is trustworthy.
